                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\delta \int_\Sigma \gamma^* A
                    \&amp; =
                    \\delta \int_\Sigma A_\mu(\gamma) \dot \gamma^\mu d \tau
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \int_\Sigma
                    \\left(
                    (\partial_{\nu} A_\mu)(\gamma) \dot \gamma^\mu
                    \-
                    \\frac{d}{d\tau} (A_\nu(\gamma))
                    \\right)
                    \\delta \gamma^\nu
                    d \tau
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \int_\Sigma
                    \\left(
                    (\partial_{\nu} A_\mu)(\gamma) \dot \gamma^\mu
                    \-
                    (\partial_\mu A_\nu)(\gamma)) \dot \gamma^\mu
                    \\right)
                    \\delta \gamma^\nu
                    d\tau
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
          %p The variation of the kinetic terms is slightly subtle due to the square root in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dvol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi γ
                        %mo ˙
                      %mo ,
                      %mover
                        %mi γ
                        %mo ˙
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi τ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  dvol(\gamma^* g) = \sqrt{g(\dot \gamma, \dot \gamma)} d\tau
                  \\,.
          %p
            To deal with this, we first look at variations of trajectories in a small region where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\dot \gamma, \dot \gamma)
            is non-zero. For such we can always find a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \stackrel{\simeq }{\to} \Sigma
            such that this term is constantly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} = 1
            in this region (recall that configurations are diffeomorphism classes of smooth
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curve"} curve
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p With the above choice of diffeomorphism gauge, the equations of motion are
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
